Class Reflection – Session 8
This session made me think about AI product management from a more practical perspective. I understood that the course is not just about learning AI concepts, 
but about learning how to use AI to build products, improve product management work, and embed AI into products themselves.
One point I found interesting was the professor’s explanation of the three pillars of AI usage. First, we can use AI tools to build products. 
Second, we can use AI to prepare and improve product management documents. Third, we can embed AI into the product so that customers experience its 
capabilities directly.
I realised that these three areas are related, but they are not the same. Using ChatGPT to prepare a product requirements document 
is different from using AI to develop the product, and both are different from offering an AI-powered feature to customers.

In my current role in automation, I see an opportunity to apply these ideas. We often focus on automating an existing process, 
but the bigger question is whether we are solving the right problem and delivering a measurable business outcome.

Another important learning was that product management is about ownership, not just coordination. A product manager needs to understand the customer,
business objectives, technology, and execution. Even when different teams contribute, the product manager must keep the overall outcome in focus.

The professor’s encouragement to maintain personal reflections also made sense to me. 
Instead of writing only what was taught, I should capture what I understood, where I disagree, how I can apply it.

For my own career, these ideas are useful as I move from automation delivery towards AI product management.
My biggest takeaway is that an AI product manager must know how to use AI, how to build with AI, 
and how to deliver value through AI—while remaining accountable for the product’s business outcomes.
